---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const world = [
  ["Italy",17819,"1:3,432"],["United States",6389,"1:56,732"],["Argentina",3424,"1:12,483"],
  ["Portugal",3057,"1:3,408"],["Angola",2958,"1:9,124"],["Spain",2852,"1:16,393"],
  ["Brazil",2700,"1:79,287"],["Philippines",1795,"1:56,400"],["France",1763,"1:37,676"],
  ["Colombia",796,"1:60,018"],["Uruguay",575,"1:5,968"],["England",460,"1:121,126"],
  ["Canada",430,"1:85,687"],["Venezuela",369,"1:81,854"],["Australia",215,"1:125,561"],
];
const variants = [
  ["Falco, De Falco, Di Falco","the South — De Falco above all Campanian, and Neapolitan"],
  ["Falcone, Falconio, Falconieri","the South"],
  ["Falchi, Falchieri","Emilia; Falchi also Sardinia"],
  ["Falcoz","Valle d'Aosta"],
  ["Falconèr","the Venetie"],
];
---
<Base title="The name">
  <h1>The name</h1>
  <p class="lede"><em>Falco</em> is the Latin and Italian for <strong>falcon</strong>. It becomes a surname
  by two quite separate roads, and telling them apart is the first problem of any Falco genealogy.</p>

  <h2>Two origins, not one</h2>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>As a Lombard personal name</h3>
      <p><em>Falco</em> was a common given name among the Lombards. Where the surname descends by this
      road it is a <strong>patronymic</strong> — the family of a man called Falco — and carries no
      suggestion of birds or hunting at all.</p>
      <p class="small">This matters here more than usual. Arienzo sits at the edge of the Valle Caudina,
      in the orbit of <strong>Benevento</strong> — the seat of the greatest of the Lombard duchies of
      southern Italy, which outlasted the northern kingdom by three hundred years.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>As a nickname or a trade</h3>
      <p>For a man quick, sharp-eyed or predatory; or for a <em>falconiere</em>, a falconer — an occupation
      of real standing in the Norman and Angevin south, where hawking was a courtly art.</p>
      <p class="small">Frederick II, who ruled this country, wrote the treatise on it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p><strong>The consequence is a rule, not a caveat.</strong> A name that can be invented anywhere will be
  invented everywhere. Two Falco families sharing nothing but the name are the norm, not the exception,
  and no Falco line may be joined to another on the strength of the surname alone.</p>

  <h2>Where the name is</h2>
  <p>Forebears counts <strong>46,749</strong> bearers worldwide, the 12,010th commonest surname on earth.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Density</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{world.map(([c,n,d]) => (
      <tr><td>{c}</td><td class="mono">{n.toLocaleString()}</td><td class="mono">{d}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small"><strong>Read the Iberian rows with care.</strong> Portugal, Angola and the Philippines
  are almost certainly a different name — Portuguese and Galician <em>Falco</em>, and Catalan
  <em>Falcó</em> — carried by Portuguese and Spanish colonial settlement, not by Italian emigration. The
  Italian diaspora is the United States, Argentina, Brazil, France, Uruguay, Venezuela, Canada and
  Australia. Folding Angola into an Italian story would be a mistake.</p>

  <h2>Within Italy</h2>
  <p>Roughly <strong>7,826 Falco families</strong>. Around 60% are in the south and the islands, 32% in the
  north, 8% in the centre. The named strongholds are <strong>Sicily</strong>, <strong>Calabria</strong> and
  <strong>Campania</strong>. The northern third is probably in large part twentieth-century internal
  migration to Milan and Turin rather than an old northern population — but that is a hypothesis, and it
  should be tested against pre-1900 records rather than assumed.</p>

  <h2>The other names in this family</h2>
  <p>A surname's rarity is itself evidence — it tells you how tightly a family is tied to one place.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Bearers worldwide</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Falco</strong></td><td class="mono">46,749</td>
        <td>Common, and independently invented many times. Proves nothing on its own.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Zampiello</strong></td><td class="mono">~123</td>
        <td>84 in the United States, <strong>37 in Italy</strong>, 2 in France — the 1,628,521st commonest
        surname on earth. Francesca Zampiello of Arpaia married Carmine Falco in 1955. A name this rare is
        effectively a single family, and the Italian remnant is small enough to be traceable in full.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Annecchino</strong></td><td class="mono">1,093 records here</td>
        <td><strong>Generation seven's wife.</strong> Filomena Annecchino married Carminantonio Falco.
        <strong>The largest surname in this archive</strong> — ahead of Falco itself since the Benevento
        harvest of 10 September 2026 — clustered hard on <strong>Moiano</strong> (214) and
        <strong>Forchia</strong> (106) at the head of the Valle Caudina.
        <a href={u("/annecchino")}>The Annecchino &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Maione / Majone</strong></td><td class="mono">69 records here</td>
        <td><strong>Generation six's wife.</strong> Costanza Maione of Arienzo, born 2 May 1876, married
        Raffaele Falco &mdash; and hers is <em>the one wife's birth in the spine with a record behind
        it</em>. Written <em>Majone</em> throughout the nineteenth-century registers.
        <a href={u("/maione")}>The Maione &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ferrara / Ferraro</strong></td><td class="mono">14 records here</td>
        <td><strong>Francesca Zampiello's mother.</strong> Giuseppa Ferrara married Angelo Zampiello.
        The family writes her <em>Ferrara</em>; every Italian civil record found so far writes her
        <strong>Ferraro</strong>. <a href={u("/ferrara")}>The Ferrara &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Crisci</strong></td><td class="mono">94 records here</td>
        <td><strong>Generations one AND three.</strong> Francesca Crisci married Matteo Falco; Andreana
        Crisci married Vincenzo. The archive counts <strong>eleven Crisci&ndash;Falco marriages</strong>
        across two centuries &mdash; six Crisci women in, five Falco women out, the last of them in the
        Brisbane generation. <a href={u("/crisci")}>The Crisci &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Rivetti</strong></td><td class="mono">148 records here</td>
        <td><strong>Generation two.</strong> Chiara Rivetti married Pasquale Falco and outlived him by
        nine years. <strong>Two Rivetti families shared the town</strong>: hers were <em>contadini</em>,
        the other had an apothecary, a physician styled <em>Doctoris Physici</em>, and three men given
        the honorific <em>Don</em>. Telling them apart took five years.
        <a href={u("/rivetti")}>The Rivetti &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Martone</strong>, <strong>Arricale</strong></td><td class="mono">375 · 9</td>
        <td>Two more Arienzo names. <strong>Giovanna Arricole married generation five</strong>, and
        <P n="Anna Martone" bold={true} /> is Costanza Maione's mother &mdash; so both touch the spine, and
        neither has been worked out yet. Martone is one of the commonest names in these registers,
        which is exactly why none of its 375 records has been joined to Anna.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">Zampiello's variants — Zampillo, Zampello, Zampella — may or may not be related; Forebears
  lists them as phonetic neighbours only, and no connection has been tested here.</p>

  <h2>The variants, and why the prefix matters</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Where it dominates</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{variants.map(([a,b]) => <tr><td><strong>{a}</strong></td><td>{b}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>De Falco is strongly Campanian.</strong> Clerks moved between <em>Falco</em> and
  <em>De Falco</em> freely, and one family can appear under both within a generation. In the Arienzo civil
  records the registrar himself is at one point a <em>Giacomo De Falco</em> — no relation established, but
  a useful reminder that the prefix is a scribal habit, not a different family.</p>
  <p class="small">FamilySearch's own index treats <em>Falco</em> and <em>Falcone</em> as variants of one
  another and returns both for either query. Convenient for searching; dangerous for concluding.</p>
</Base>
